  "text": "Certainly! Here's a review of *The Great Gatsby* that features a number of proper nouns from the book, which will contribute to the evaluation:\n\n---\n\n**Title:** *The Great Gatsby* \u2013 A Timeless Reflection on Illusion and Ambition\n\nF. Scott Fitzgerald's *The Great Gatsby* is a literary masterpiece that captures the essence of the American Dream through the eyes of the enigmatic Jay Gatsby. Set in the roaring 1920s, the novel takes place in West Egg and East Egg, two parts of Long Island that symbolize the divide between old money and new wealth. The story centers around Jay Gatsby, a mysterious millionaire whose lavish parties are known to attract the city's elite, including Daisy Buchanan, the object of his affection.\n\nGatsby's obsession with Daisy, who is married to Tom Buchanan, drives the narrative forward. Tom, with his imposing presence and aristocratic background, represents the entrenched privilege of the East Egg society. His affair with Myrtle Wilson, the wife of George Wilson, adds a layer of tragedy and social dissonance to the tale.\n\nThe story also features the enigmatic Nick Carraway, the novel's narrator, who serves as both a participant and a witness to the unfolding drama. Nick's relationship with Jordan Baker, a worldly and independent woman, further complicates the emotional landscape of the novel.\n\nOne of the most haunting moments in the novel occurs in the Valley of Ashes, a desolate area that contrasts sharply with the opulence of West Egg. The tragic ending of Gatsby, revealed in the aftermath of a fatal car accident, underscores the novel's central theme: the futility of pursuing an idealized past.\n\n*The Great Gatsby* is not just a story about love and loss; it is a profound commentary on the American Dream, the corruption of wealth, and the illusion of happiness. The novel's title, *The Great Gatsby*, refers not only to the man himself but also to the dream he represents\u2014a dream that ultimately remains just out of reach.\n\n---\n\n**Proper Nouns from the Book:**\n\n1. The Great Gatsby  \n2. Jay Gatsby  \n3. Daisy Buchanan  \n4. Tom Buchanan  \n5. Myrtle Wilson  \n6. George Wilson  \n7. Nick Carraway  \n8. Jordan Baker  \n9. West Egg  \n10. East Egg  \n11. Long Island  \n12. Valley of Ashes  \n13. Gatsby  \n\nLet me know if you'd like a version with exactly a specific number of proper nouns or a different tone!",
